19:T4ad,## Many shapes, one name

**Polymorphism** (“many forms”) lets you call **one method** on a reference and have the **right implementation** run for whatever object is actually there.

The kind that matters for design is **subtype polymorphism**: a supertype declares a method, and each subtype **overrides** it.

```
Shape s = pickRandomShape();
s.area();   // which area()? decided at RUNTIME
```

## Dynamic dispatch

The compiler doesn't pick the method, the **runtime** does, by looking at the object's real class. This is **dynamic dispatch** (a.k.a. late binding). A `Circle` runs `Circle.area()`; a `Square` runs `Square.area()`same call site.

## Why it's the heart of OOP design

It lets you **program to an interface, not an implementation**:

```
double total = 0;
for (Shape s : shapes) total += s.area();   // no if/else per type!
```

Add a `Pentagon` tomorrow and this loop never changes, that's the **Open/Closed Principle** in action. Polymorphism is what makes the Strategy, State, and Template-Method patterns work.

> Not to be confused with **overloading** (same name, different parameter lists, chosen at *compile* time), that's ad-hoc polymorphism and far less powerful.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"polymorphism"}],["$","$L18","polymorphism",{"lesson":{"id":"polymorphism","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"Polymorphism","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"One call, many forms: the SAME method name resolves to different behaviour depending on the actual object, decided at runtime.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"One interface, many implementations","code":["interface Shape { double area(); }","","class Circle   implements Shape { double area(){ return PI*r*r; } }","class Square   implements Shape { double area(){ return s*s; } }","class Triangle implements Shape { double area(){ return b*h/2; } }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"A supertype declares the contract: every Shape promises an area() method, but says nothing about HOW.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"shape","name":"Shape","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ area(): double"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"circle","name":"Circle","fields":["- r: double"],"methods":["+ area()"],"x":0,"y":1.7},{"id":"square","name":"Square","fields":["- s: double"],"methods":["+ area()"],"x":1,"y":1.7},{"id":"tri","name":"Triangle","fields":["- b, h"],"methods":["+ area()"],"x":2,"y":1.7}],"relations":[{"id":"rc","from":"circle","to":"shape","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rs","from":"square","to":"shape","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rt","from":"tri","to":"shape","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["shape"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["shape"]},"vars":[["contract","area(): double"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Circle implements Shape and gives area() its OWN body: π·r². It overrides the contract.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","circle"],"shownRelIds":["rc"],"activeIds":["circle"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Square overrides area() differently: side². Same method name, different code.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","circle","square"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rs"],"activeIds":["square"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"Triangle too: ½·b·h. Three classes, one shared interface, each free to compute area its own way.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","circle","square","tri"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rs","rt"],"activeIds":["tri"]}},{"view":"class","narration":"Now any code can hold a Shape reference and call area() without knowing or caring which concrete shape it is. That decoupling is the whole point.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","circle","square","tri"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rs","rt"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["program to","the interface"]]}]},{"label":"Dynamic dispatch at runtime","code":["for (Shape s : shapes)","    total += s.area();   // ← same line, different method runs"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":1,"narration":"The Renderer holds Shapes but treats them all the same, it just calls area() in a loop. Watch the SAME call land on different objects.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"r","label":"Renderer","kind":"object"},{"id":"c","label":"aCircle","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"s","label":"aSquare","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"t","label":"aTriangle","tint":"accent2"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"r","to":"c","label":"area()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"c","to":"r","label":"78.5","kind":"return"},{"id":"m3","from":"r","to":"s","label":"area()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m4","from":"s","to":"r","label":"12.25","kind":"return"},{"id":"m5","from":"r","to":"t","label":"area()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m6","from":"t","to":"r","label":"6.0","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":0,"activeIndex":-1},"vars":[["call site","s.area()"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"First element is a Circle. s.area() dispatches to Circle.area() → 78.5. The runtime chose, not the compiler.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["runtime type","Circle"],["→","78.5"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"Next element is a Square. The IDENTICAL call s.area() now runs Square.area() → 12.25.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["runtime type","Square"],["→","12.25"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"And a Triangle → 6.0. One line of code, three different methods executed, chosen by each object's real type. That is dynamic dispatch.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":6,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["runtime type","Triangle"],["→","6.0"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"Add a Pentagon class tomorrow and this loop never changes. New behaviour slots in without touching the caller, polymorphism is what makes code extensible.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":6,"activeIndex":-1},"vars":[["add Pentagon","loop unchanged ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
